        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The Spring TestContext Framework (located in the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "org.springframework.test.context"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " package) provides generic, annotation-driven unit and integration testing support that is agnostic of the testing framework in use. The TestContext framework also places a great deal of importance on convention over configuration, with reasonable defaults that you can override through annotation-based configuration."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "In addition to generic testing infrastructure, the TestContext framework provides explicit support for JUnit 4, JUnit Jupiter (AKA JUnit 5), and TestNG. For JUnit 4 and TestNG, Spring provides "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "abstract"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " support classes. Furthermore, Spring provides a custom JUnit "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Runner"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and custom JUnit "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Rules"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " for JUnit 4 and a custom "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Extension"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " for JUnit Jupiter that let you write so-called POJO test classes. POJO test classes are not required to extend a particular class hierarchy, such as the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
